    <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-4">Fellbagger privacy policy</h1>

    <p><strong>Last updated:</strong> 3 November 2026</p>

    <p>Fellbagger is made by Fellside Digital ("we", "us"). This policy explains what the app does with information about you. The short version: almost everything stays on your phone, and we have designed it that way on purpose.</p>

    <p>If you have any questions, email <strong><a href="mailto:oliver.lewington@fellsidedigital.co.uk">oliver.lewington@fellsidedigital.co.uk</a></strong>.</p>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>

    <p>Fellside Digital, Cumbria, United Kingdom, is the data controller for anything described here. Contact: <a href="mailto:oliver.lewington@fellsidedigital.co.uk">oliver.lewington@fellsidedigital.co.uk</a>.</p>

    <h2>What stays on your phone</h2>

    <p>Fellbagger has no accounts of its own. Unless you choose to connect Strava or Garmin Connect (both described below), there is no sign-in at all. The following is stored only on your device and is never sent to us:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>Your ascent log — which fells you have bagged and when</li>
        <li>Your recorded walks, including the GPS track of each walk</li>
        <li>Your settings, and whether you have bought Fellbagger Pro</li>
        <li>The app's diagnostics log (used only if you choose to copy it and send it to us yourself)</li>
    </ul>

    <p>Because we never receive this information, we cannot see it, recover it, or delete it for you. <strong>Deleting the app deletes all of it.</strong> You can export any walk or route as a GPX file at any time if you want a copy.</p>

    <h2>Location</h2>

    <p>The app uses your phone's location to:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>Show where you are on the map and how far you are from each fell</li>
        <li>Record the track of a walk while you are recording one</li>
        <li>Bag summits automatically as you reach them (a Fellbagger Pro feature)</li>
        <li>Show the weather where you are, and sunsets, clear nights and other outdoor events near you</li>
    </ul>

    <p>While you are recording a walk, the app keeps using your location with your phone in your pocket or the screen off, so the recording continues and summits are still detected. It only does this for a walk you started, and it stops when you end the walk. The app never asks for location "all the time" and never uses your location when you aren't recording or using it. Where your phone has a barometer, the app also reads it on the phone to measure ascent more accurately than GPS alone.</p>

    <p><strong>Your precise location stays on your phone.</strong> It is never sent to us, and walks you record in Fellbagger, with their GPS tracks, never leave your phone. Activities you choose to bring in from Garmin Connect work differently and reach your phone through our server, as described under <em>Connecting Garmin Connect</em>. To show the weather where you are and outdoor events near you, the app sends an <strong>approximate</strong> position — rounded to about 1 km — to the weather service (Open-Meteo, below), and your phone's own place-name lookup (Apple's or Google's) uses the same approximate position to show the name of the nearest town. Nothing sent with it identifies you. For event alerts, the app remembers the approximate position it last used, on your phone, so it can check the forecast there later; it never tracks where you are in the background for this.</p>

    <p>You can turn location access off at any time in your phone's settings; the app still works for logging fells by hand.</p>

    <h2>What leaves your phone, and where it goes</h2>

    <p>Some features need the internet. Each is listed below with exactly what is sent. Apart from the Strava and Garmin connections you choose to make, none of them includes your name, an account, or your ascent history.</p>

